From: Tim auf der Landwehr <tadl-git@taufderl.de>
Subject: [PATCH] mode: align the row stride to 16 bytes, not 8

libmegapixels pads a raw row up to the next multiple of 8 bytes. The
Qualcomm CAMSS driver pads to 16, and the two disagree whenever the packed
row length is a multiple of 8 but not of 16 -- at which point megapixels
computes a frame size 8 bytes per row short of what the driver produces and
dies on its own assertion:

  mp_camera_capture_buffer: Assertion `bytesused ==
      (libmegapixels_mode_width_to_bytes(format, width)
       + libmegapixels_mode_width_to_padding(format, width)) * height' failed

Measured on a Fairphone 4 (sm7225, qcom-camss). Its rear sensor offers a
fixed 4000x2256 mode, and 4000 px of packed 10-bit is 5000 bytes:

  5000 % 8  == 0   ->  libmegapixels pads 0, expects 5000/row
  5000 % 16 == 8   ->  the driver pads 8, produces 5008/row

which VIDIOC_G_FMT confirms as `Bytes per Line : 5008`, and a capture
confirms again: three frames of 33894144 bytes total is exactly
5008 * 2256 * 3.

This is invisible on every device supported so far because their widths
happen to be 16-byte clean. The Pixel 3a's 4032 px is 5040 bytes and the
PinePhone's 3264 px is 4080, both multiples of 16, so the two rules agree
and no device has yet disagreed with this one.

Aligning to 16 is a superset: any row already 16-aligned is unchanged, so
no currently working device moves.

The tidier fix is for megapixels to take the stride from the
`bytesperline` V4L2 already reports rather than recompute it, since the
driver is the authority on its own padding. That is a larger change than
this, and this is what makes the camera work today.
---
--- a/src/mode.c
+++ b/src/mode.c
@@ -330,10 +330,10 @@
 	}
 	uint64_t bytes_per_width = libmegapixels_mode_width_to_bytes(index, width);
-	uint64_t remainder = bytes_per_width % 8;
+	uint64_t remainder = bytes_per_width % 16;
 	if (remainder == 0)
 		return remainder;
 
-	return 8 - remainder;
+	return 16 - remainder;
 }
